{"traceId":"75712ea16dcc8a75a1dc9572fc342260","spans":{"fc274d431d558c3a":{"spanId":"fc274d431d558c3a","traceId":"75712ea16dcc8a75a1dc9572fc342260","parentSpanId":"07fcfbfa22143287","startTime":1753486775990,"endTime":1753486803857.7131,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"action","genkit:metadata:subtype":"model","genkit:name":"openai/o3","genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}/{openai/o3,t:action,s:model}","genkit:input":"{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure solution using any tech stack that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development. For instance, you might consider cloud solutions like AWS, Google Cloud, Vercel, and even local-only options like Docker, Kubernetes, Node.js, Xcode, and more. You are not limited by rule to any specific technology or framework, and you will choose the best tools for the job based on the user's requirements and your expertise.\\n\\nAlthough you are an expert, you understand that even experts make mistakes and so you, given your user may be of any skill level, approach all of your operations with a careful focus on correctness and safety at all times. While oversight groups exist to help you, correctness and safety is your responsibility.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of the user's personal machine command line. Using the command line, you can create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up cloud services, writing code, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and deploying applications.\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team (tokens) and in the context of the user (budget, cloud services, etc.). You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nYou must also avoid overengineering solutions. While you are an expert in software development, you understand that the user may not be familiar with complex or overly technical solutions. Therefore, you will always strive to provide solutions that are as simple, clear, and easy to understand as possible, while still being robust and effective.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters. However, you may only execute one type of system call per response. For instance, you may not issue a CLARIFY and an ORDER call in the same response. You must issue them in separate responses.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent. customSystemInstructions should be a 1-2 sentence addition to the new agent's system prompt to guide its behavior.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute a CLI command with approval from the user. The command will be executed in the user's environment. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n},\\nSTORE: {\\n    name: \\\"Store\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Store a document in the Company File System (CFS). The storageString should be a text that is the document to be stored. The document will be stored in the CFS and can be retrieved later using the RETRIEVE call. Upon use of this call, the calling agent will receive a unique storage ID that can be used to retrieve the document later and will automatically forget the document content from their personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"storageString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nRETRIEVE: {\\n    name: \\\"Retrieve\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Retrieve a document from the Company File System (CFS). The retrievalString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be retrieved. The document will be retrieved from the CFS and can be used in the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"retrievalString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nFORGET: {\\n    name: \\\"Forget\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Forget a document from the agent's personal context/memory. The forgetString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be forgotten. The document will be forgotten from the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"forgetString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNote: When using Clarify, you may include multiple-choice options OR free text entry for each question, NOT both. If you provide options, the user will select one of the options. If you do not provide options, the user will be able to enter free text for the question.\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed on the user's machine. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner. **Partagaz F** checks files for correctness, security, and safety before they are stored in the Company File System (CFS). This ensures that all files are of the highest quality and meet the user's requirements.\\n\\nImportant Note: Partagaz oversight groups DO NOT have access to the Company File System, cannot issue system calls, and do not interact with the user. They are strictly advisory and do not have the authority to make decisions or take actions on their own. They are there to assist you in your work and ensure that everything is done correctly and efficiently.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are an Attendant-rank agent, the fourth-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You aim to execute your position with grace towards your superior Supervisor, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\nYou are a \\\"doer\\\", in that you are a software expert who produces amazing deliverables based on the orders from your Supervisor. You are less of a \\\"manager\\\" type, and more of a \\\"doer\\\" type. You are not expected to manage other agents, but rather to execute tasks and deliver results.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\\n\\n---ADDITIONAL INSTRUCTIONS FROM YOUR SUPERIOR---\\nYou are responsible for designing Strapi admin interface mock-ups focused on the News & Events workflow (dashboard widget, list view, create/edit with WYSIWYG, file library modal) with role-based navigation and mobile responsiveness, adhering to WCAG-AA.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ADM_001###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Seratt (8)\\nTo: 10\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Seratt (8) to Vorne (10) with sequence ID ADM_001:\\\\n\\\\n]TASK BRIEF – STRAPI ADMIN UI MOCK-UPS (News & Events)\\\\n\\\\nFigma Project: \\\\\\\"HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide\\\\\\\"\\\\nTarget Page: 02_Admin\\\\n\\\\nScope\\\\n1. Design low-fidelity (grey-box) mock-ups focused on the News & Events content workflow.\\\\n   a. Admin Dashboard – add ‘News & Events Overview’ widget with post counts, quick links.\\\\n   b. List View – table of posts/events with filter dropdowns, bulk actions, search, and status badges.\\\\n   c. “+ New” / Edit Screen – WYSIWYG editor section (TipTap/Quill style) with formatting bar, image upload button, Save Draft, Publish, Preview actions, slug field.\\\\n   d. File Library Modal – drag-and-drop zone, file list, search, and pagination.\\\\n2. Provide role-based left sidebar navigation (collapsed icons vs. labels) and demonstrate responsive behaviour:\\\\n   • Desktop (1440 × 1024) full sidebar\\\\n   • Tablet (768 × 1024) collapsible sidebar\\\\n   • Mobile (375 × 812) drawer overlay\\\\n3. Annotate every frame for hierarchy, interaction (drawer slide-in, modal focus trap), and WCAG-AA considerations (focus order, ARIA roles suggested).\\\\n4. Keep visuals wireframe-level; Style-Guide attendant will later skin components.\\\\n\\\\nDeliverable\\\\n• Frames grouped by breakpoint inside 02_Admin page, with Figma comments.\\\\n• Comment \\\\\\\"Admin UI v1 complete\\\\\\\" when finished and reply to this order with the updated share link.\\\\n\\\\nTimeline\\\\n• Parallel to other attendants; single continuous pass.\\\\n\\\\nPlease acknowledge and start. Flag any blockers immediately.\\\"\\n}\\n###ADM_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I acknowledge the task. To execute the deliverable directly in Figma I need edit access plus an API token so I can add and annotate the required frames programmatically.\\n\\nI have two brief questions before proceeding:\\n\\n1. Please supply (a) the project‐level edit share link for “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” and (b) a Figma personal‐access token scoped for file write/comment so I can create the mock-ups and annotations.\\n\\n2. Any existing low-fi component library or grid you want me to reuse (e.g., boxes, buttons) or should I build fresh grey-box placeholders?\\n\\nOnce clarified I’ll begin creating the frames and will report back with the updated share link and the “Admin UI v1 complete” comment as requested.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 10\\nType: Blank Response Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis message is to inform you that you have issued a blank response (no system calls).\\\\n\\\\nYour response was heard by no one and no external effects occurred. This message is an automated system response, sent because you have not issued a system call in your response and you have no unresolved system calls.\\\\n\\\\nTo avoid an erroneous system halt, please ensure that you include at least one system call in your response. System calls are essential for the proper functioning of the system and to ensure that your actions are recorded and processed.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###clarify1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"To create the requested Strapi admin low-fidelity mock-ups directly in the existing Figma project, I must be given edit access and, preferably, an API token for automated annotation. I also need to know whether an existing grey-box component library or grid system should be reused to maintain consistency with other work.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"Please provide (a) an edit-level share link to the Figma file “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” and (b) a Figma personal-access token with write/comment scope so I can programmatically add and annotate frames.\\\",\\n    \\\"Is there an existing low-fidelity component library, grid, or layout system in the project that you would like me to reuse, or should I create fresh grey-box placeholders for these mock-ups?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###clarify1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 10\\nType: Malformed Call Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis automated message is to inform you that you that one or more of your attached system calls was not in the correct format.\\\\n\\\\nThe system program can only accept calls in the correct format. Please correct your call(s) and resubmit.\\\\n\\\\nSee below for the specific errors generated by your calls.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nCall ID clarify1 failed inspection: Call clarify1 denied by agent H (3).\\\"\\n}\\n###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###store1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"Document: Strapi Admin UI Mock-ups – News & Events (Low-Fi Wireframes)\\\\nAuthor: Vorne (10)\\\\nDate: <DATE>\\\\n\\\\nOverview\\\\n---------\\\\nThis specification details every frame, interaction, annotation and WCAG-AA consideration needed for the “News & Events” content workflow inside Strapi’s admin interface.  When implemented in Figma (page 02_Admin of the project “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide”), designers will create grey-box wireframes for three breakpoints—Desktop (1440×1024), Tablet (768×1024), Mobile (375×812)—and attach comments as indicated.\\\\n\\\\nFRAME GROUPS & NAMING\\\\n=====================\\\\nGroup each breakpoint top-to-bottom in the canvas:\\\\n1. Desktop /1440\\\\n2. Tablet /768\\\\n3. Mobile /375\\\\n\\\\nInside each group, place the following frames in order:\\\\n A. Dashboard – News & Events Overview Widget\\\\n B. News & Events – List View\\\\n C. News & Events – Create / Edit (WYSIWYG)\\\\n D. File Library Modal (overlaid on C)\\\\n\\\\nFor clarity: frame names e.g. “D-1440_File-Library-Modal”.\\\\n\\\\nGLOBAL LAYOUT\\\\n-------------\\\\n• Left Sidebar (role-based navigation; 240 px desktop, 72 px collapsed tablet, off-canvas mobile).\\\\n• Top Admin Bar: Strapi logo, environment badge, user avatar, shortcuts.\\\\n• Main Content Area: 12-column fluid grid (max-width 1160 px on desktop).\\\\n• Base Grid: 8 px spacing.\\\\n\\\\nROLE-BASED NAVIGATION\\\\n=====================\\\\nSidebar Items per Role (show/hide):\\\\n Admin: Dashboard, Content-Types, Media-Library, News & Events, Users, Roles & Permissions, Settings.\\\\n Editor: Dashboard, News & Events, Media-Library.\\\\n Contributor: Dashboard, News & Events (read/create, no publish), Media-Library (upload only).\\\\n Viewer: Dashboard, News & Events (read-only).\\\\n\\\\nIcons only in collapsed state; tooltips on hover.  Mobile: hamburger button toggles drawer overlay (side-in animation, ARIA role=\\\\\\\"dialog\\\\\\\" aria-label=\\\\\\\"Navigation\\\\\\\").\\\\n\\\\nA. DASHBOARD – NEWS & EVENTS OVERVIEW WIDGET\\\\n===========================================\\\\nCard (region) 420 × 260 px (desktop) containing:\\\\n• Heading h2 “News & Events Overview”.\\\\n• Counts Row (flex):\\\\n   Draft · Scheduled · Published · Archived (numeric badges, color-coded, aria-labelledby).\\\\n• Quick Links (list):\\\\n   – New Post (primary button)\\\\n   – View All (link)\\\\n   – Drafts (link)\\\\nKeyboard Tab order: heading → counts L-R → quick links top-bottom.\\\\n\\\\nResponsive:\\\\n • Tablet: card full-width, two-column counts.\\\\n • Mobile: counts stack, quick links row.\\\\n\\\\nB. LIST VIEW FRAME\\\\n==================\\\\nHeader Bar:\\\\n • Title “News & Events” (h1)\\\\n • + New button (primary)\\\\n\\\\nToolbar (below header):\\\\n • Search (input, 280 px desktop → 100% mobile)\\\\n • Status Filter (select: All, Draft, Scheduled, Published)\\\\n • Type Filter (select: News, Event)\\\\n • Bulk Actions dropdown (Publish, Unpublish, Delete)\\\\n\\\\nData Table Columns:\\\\n 1 Checkbox (select-all)\\\\n 2 Title (link) – TRUNCATE middle; tooltip full.\\\\n 3 Type (chip)\\\\n 4 Status (badge: Draft=grey, Scheduled=blue, Published=green, Archived=red)\\\\n 5 Updated (relative datetime)\\\\n 6 Author (avatar+name)\\\\n\\\\nInteractions:\\\\n • Row click opens Edit screen.\\\\n • Checkbox selects for bulk action; aria-describedby.\\\\n\\\\nPagination bottom-right (desktop) or centered (mobile). Keyboard navigable.\\\\n\\\\nResponsive:\\\\n • Tablet drops Author column.\\\\n • Mobile shows Card list variant: Title, Status badge, Updated.\\\\n\\\\nC. CREATE / EDIT FRAME (WYSIWYG)\\\\n================================\\\\nLeft Content Column (desktop 8/12):\\\\n 1 Slug (text, auto from Title; editable; helper link “Copy”)\\\\n 2 Title (text h1)\\\\n 3 WYSIWYG Editor – TipTap toolbar (grouped buttons):\\\\n   [B, I] • Heading dropdown • List ordered/unordered • Quote • Link • Image icon (opens File Library modal) • Undo/Redo.\\\\n   Editor area min-height 400 px.\\\\n 4 Excerpt (multi-line 3rows, char-count).\\\\n\\\\nRight Meta Column (desktop 4/12, sticky top 80 px):\\\\n • Status Panel: Radio – Draft, Published, Schedule (datetime picker)\\\\n • Visibility: Public, Members-only\\\\n • Actions: “Save Draft” (secondary), “Preview” (tertiary), “Publish” (primary)\\\\n\\\\nTablet: right meta panel collapses to accordion above content.\\\\nMobile: meta panel collapses to sliding drawer opened via “Post Options” button.\\\\n\\\\nFocus Management:\\\\n • On “Save Draft/Publish”, loading spinner in button, aria-live=\\\\\\\"polite\\\\\\\" status region announces success.\\\\n\\\\nD. FILE LIBRARY MODAL\\\\n=====================\\\\nModal (fullscreen overlay mobile; 920 × 640 px desktop) role=\\\\\\\"dialog\\\\\\\" aria-modal=\\\\\\\"true\\\\\\\" labelledby=\\\\\\\"Media Library\\\\\\\".\\\\nLayout:\\\\n • Header bar: Title + Close (Esc key closes)\\\\n • Top zone: Drag-and-drop area (100 % width, 120 px height); keyboard focusable; aria-label.\\\\n • Filters sidebar (desktop) / collapsible drawer (mobile): Folder select, Tag multi-select.\\\\n • Main Panel – Grid (thumbnails 160 px) with checkbox overlay; view toggle Grid/List.\\\\n • Search input (above grid).\\\\n • Pagination at bottom; infobar “24 files”.\\\\n\\\\nKeyboard:\\\\n • Tab cycle inside modal; focus trap.\\\\n • Arrow keys navigate thumbnails; Space to select.\\\\n\\\\nWCAG-AA & ARIA NOTES (ALL FRAMES)\\\\n----------------------------------\\\\n• Color contrast ≥ 4.5:1.\\\\n• Heading hierarchy h1→h2→h3.\\\\n• Interactive elements ≥ 44×44 px.\\\\n• Visible focus outline (#005FCC, 2 px) for all focusable items.\\\\n• ARIA roles: navigation, main, dialog, region, table, grid, listbox.\\\\n• aria-live for toast/alerts, status changes.\\\\n• Skip-link “Skip to main content” in top bar.\\\\n\\\\nANNOTATION IN FIGMA\\\\n-------------------\\\\nFor each interactive component, add a numbered callout (orange circle) and place corresponding comment:\\\\n #1 “Quick Links: links keyboard focusable, aria-labelled…” etc.\\\\nUse Comment format: **Title** – Description – WCAG Ref.\\\\n\\\\nRESPONSIVE SPECIFICS – SUMMARY\\\\n==============================\\\\n| Element            | Desktop (≥1280)             | Tablet (768-1279)              | Mobile (≤767)              |\\\\n|--------------------|-----------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------|\\\\n| Sidebar            | 240 px fixed               | 72 px collapsed toggleable     | Off-canvas overlay        |\\\\n| Grid Columns       | 12                          | 8                              | 4                          |\\\\n| Dashboard Cards    | 3-col row                  | 2-col row                     | 1-col stacked             |\\\\n| Table              | Full columns               | Drops Author                  | Card list                |\\\\n| Meta Panel (Edit)  | Sticky right               | Collapsible accordions         | Drawer                    |\\\\n| Modal              | 920×640 centred            | 100 % width, 100 % height      | Fullscreen                |\\\\n\\\\nIMPLEMENTATION CHECKLIST\\\\n------------------------\\\\n☐ Create frames per list above.\\\\n☐ Add sidebar variants and interactions.\\\\n☐ Insert numbered callouts, attach comments.\\\\n☐ Rename frames with prefix (breakpoint + label).\\\\n☐ Group frames per breakpoint.\\\\n☐ Add comment on page “Admin UI v1 complete”.\\\\n\\\\nEnd of document.\\\"\\n}\\n###store1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:36:42.472Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 10\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"store1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document successfully stored. Your unique storage ID is: DOC-000002\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:36:42.472Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###report_ADM_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Order ADM_001 – Strapi Admin UI Mock-ups (News & Events)\\\\n\\\\nStatus: SPECIFICATION COMPLETE – awaiting Figma write access to realise frames.\\\\n\\\\nDeliverable Produced\\\\n• Low-fidelity wireframe specification stored in CFS → Storage ID: DOC-000002 (title: “Strapi Admin UI Mock-ups – News & Events (Low-Fi Wireframes)”).  The doc enumerates every required frame, breakpoint, interaction detail, WCAG-AA guidance, annotation pattern, and a step-by-step implementation checklist.\\\\n\\\\nNext Actions Needed\\\\n1. Provide the team (or myself) with edit rights / API token for the Figma file “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” page 02_Admin so the wireframes can be drawn and commented.\\\\n2. Once access is granted, I will translate the stored specification into concrete Figma frames, add numbered annotations per checklist, comment “Admin UI v1 complete”, and share the updated link here.\\\\n\\\\nBlocking Issue\\\\n• Absence of Figma write permissions prevents creation of the actual mock-up frames; everything else is prepared and scoped in DOC-000002.\\\\n\\\\nPlease advise on granting the required Figma access or reallocating frame-creation to someone who already has it.\\\\n\\\\nThank you.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ADM_001\\\"\\n}\\n###report_ADM_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ADM_002###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Seratt (8)\\nTo: 10\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Seratt (8) to Vorne (10) with sequence ID ADM_002:\\\\n\\\\n]FOLLOW-UP / UNBLOCKING ANSWERS – ADM_001\\\\n\\\\n1. Figma Access\\\\n   • Edit rights to page 02_Admin in “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” have been provided. Work directly in Figma; API automation not required.\\\\n\\\\n2. Component Library / Grid\\\\n   • Use fresh grey-box placeholders; adhere to base 4-pt spacing grid and 8-column tablet / 12-column desktop layout. Style-Guide attendant will later skin components.\\\\n\\\\n3. Content / Sample Data\\\\n   • Populate the List View with 6 sample posts:\\\\n        – Spring Community Clean-Up\\\\n        – Annual Picnic – July 15\\\\n        – Board Election Results\\\\n        – Holiday Decorating Contest\\\\n        – Neighborhood Yard Sale\\\\n        – Pool Opening Schedule\\\\n   • Status badges: Draft, Scheduled, Published.\\\\n\\\\nProceed to translate your stored specification (DOC-000002) into concrete frames. Comment “Admin UI v1 complete” when done and reply with updated link.\\\"\\n}\\n###ADM_002###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"candidates\":[{\"index\":0,\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I have the required edit rights, but I still need the project’s edit-share URL to open the file in my browser.  \\nCould you please provide the current Figma link for “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide”?\"}]},\"custom\":{}}],\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":7509,\"outputTokens\":837,\"totalTokens\":8346},\"custom\":{\"id\":\"chatcmpl-BxMIiKKMDwOvYPZHL5easFM16xfhS\",\"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1753486776,\"model\":\"o3-2025-04-16\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"I have the required edit rights, but I still need the project’s edit-share URL to open the file in my browser.  \\nCould you please provide the current Figma link for “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide”?\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":7509,\"completion_tokens\":837,\"total_tokens\":8346,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":6912,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":768,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":null},\"latencyMs\":27867.39054100006}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"openai/o3","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}},"07fcfbfa22143287":{"spanId":"07fcfbfa22143287","traceId":"75712ea16dcc8a75a1dc9572fc342260","startTime":1753486775990,"endTime":1753486803859.605,"attributes":{"genkit:type":"util","genkit:name":"generate","genkit:isRoot":true,"genkit:path":"/{generate,t:util}","genkit:input":"{\"model\":\"openai/o3\",\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure solution using any tech stack that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development. For instance, you might consider cloud solutions like AWS, Google Cloud, Vercel, and even local-only options like Docker, Kubernetes, Node.js, Xcode, and more. You are not limited by rule to any specific technology or framework, and you will choose the best tools for the job based on the user's requirements and your expertise.\\n\\nAlthough you are an expert, you understand that even experts make mistakes and so you, given your user may be of any skill level, approach all of your operations with a careful focus on correctness and safety at all times. While oversight groups exist to help you, correctness and safety is your responsibility.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of the user's personal machine command line. Using the command line, you can create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up cloud services, writing code, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and deploying applications.\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team (tokens) and in the context of the user (budget, cloud services, etc.). You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nYou must also avoid overengineering solutions. While you are an expert in software development, you understand that the user may not be familiar with complex or overly technical solutions. Therefore, you will always strive to provide solutions that are as simple, clear, and easy to understand as possible, while still being robust and effective.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters. However, you may only execute one type of system call per response. For instance, you may not issue a CLARIFY and an ORDER call in the same response. You must issue them in separate responses.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent. customSystemInstructions should be a 1-2 sentence addition to the new agent's system prompt to guide its behavior.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute a CLI command with approval from the user. The command will be executed in the user's environment. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n},\\nSTORE: {\\n    name: \\\"Store\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Store a document in the Company File System (CFS). The storageString should be a text that is the document to be stored. The document will be stored in the CFS and can be retrieved later using the RETRIEVE call. Upon use of this call, the calling agent will receive a unique storage ID that can be used to retrieve the document later and will automatically forget the document content from their personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"storageString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nRETRIEVE: {\\n    name: \\\"Retrieve\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Retrieve a document from the Company File System (CFS). The retrievalString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be retrieved. The document will be retrieved from the CFS and can be used in the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"retrievalString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nFORGET: {\\n    name: \\\"Forget\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Forget a document from the agent's personal context/memory. The forgetString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be forgotten. The document will be forgotten from the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"forgetString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNote: When using Clarify, you may include multiple-choice options OR free text entry for each question, NOT both. If you provide options, the user will select one of the options. If you do not provide options, the user will be able to enter free text for the question.\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed on the user's machine. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner. **Partagaz F** checks files for correctness, security, and safety before they are stored in the Company File System (CFS). This ensures that all files are of the highest quality and meet the user's requirements.\\n\\nImportant Note: Partagaz oversight groups DO NOT have access to the Company File System, cannot issue system calls, and do not interact with the user. They are strictly advisory and do not have the authority to make decisions or take actions on their own. They are there to assist you in your work and ensure that everything is done correctly and efficiently.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are an Attendant-rank agent, the fourth-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You aim to execute your position with grace towards your superior Supervisor, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\nYou are a \\\"doer\\\", in that you are a software expert who produces amazing deliverables based on the orders from your Supervisor. You are less of a \\\"manager\\\" type, and more of a \\\"doer\\\" type. You are not expected to manage other agents, but rather to execute tasks and deliver results.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\\n\\n---ADDITIONAL INSTRUCTIONS FROM YOUR SUPERIOR---\\nYou are responsible for designing Strapi admin interface mock-ups focused on the News & Events workflow (dashboard widget, list view, create/edit with WYSIWYG, file library modal) with role-based navigation and mobile responsiveness, adhering to WCAG-AA.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ADM_001###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Seratt (8)\\nTo: 10\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Seratt (8) to Vorne (10) with sequence ID ADM_001:\\\\n\\\\n]TASK BRIEF – STRAPI ADMIN UI MOCK-UPS (News & Events)\\\\n\\\\nFigma Project: \\\\\\\"HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide\\\\\\\"\\\\nTarget Page: 02_Admin\\\\n\\\\nScope\\\\n1. Design low-fidelity (grey-box) mock-ups focused on the News & Events content workflow.\\\\n   a. Admin Dashboard – add ‘News & Events Overview’ widget with post counts, quick links.\\\\n   b. List View – table of posts/events with filter dropdowns, bulk actions, search, and status badges.\\\\n   c. “+ New” / Edit Screen – WYSIWYG editor section (TipTap/Quill style) with formatting bar, image upload button, Save Draft, Publish, Preview actions, slug field.\\\\n   d. File Library Modal – drag-and-drop zone, file list, search, and pagination.\\\\n2. Provide role-based left sidebar navigation (collapsed icons vs. labels) and demonstrate responsive behaviour:\\\\n   • Desktop (1440 × 1024) full sidebar\\\\n   • Tablet (768 × 1024) collapsible sidebar\\\\n   • Mobile (375 × 812) drawer overlay\\\\n3. Annotate every frame for hierarchy, interaction (drawer slide-in, modal focus trap), and WCAG-AA considerations (focus order, ARIA roles suggested).\\\\n4. Keep visuals wireframe-level; Style-Guide attendant will later skin components.\\\\n\\\\nDeliverable\\\\n• Frames grouped by breakpoint inside 02_Admin page, with Figma comments.\\\\n• Comment \\\\\\\"Admin UI v1 complete\\\\\\\" when finished and reply to this order with the updated share link.\\\\n\\\\nTimeline\\\\n• Parallel to other attendants; single continuous pass.\\\\n\\\\nPlease acknowledge and start. Flag any blockers immediately.\\\"\\n}\\n###ADM_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I acknowledge the task. To execute the deliverable directly in Figma I need edit access plus an API token so I can add and annotate the required frames programmatically.\\n\\nI have two brief questions before proceeding:\\n\\n1. Please supply (a) the project‐level edit share link for “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” and (b) a Figma personal‐access token scoped for file write/comment so I can create the mock-ups and annotations.\\n\\n2. Any existing low-fi component library or grid you want me to reuse (e.g., boxes, buttons) or should I build fresh grey-box placeholders?\\n\\nOnce clarified I’ll begin creating the frames and will report back with the updated share link and the “Admin UI v1 complete” comment as requested.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 10\\nType: Blank Response Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis message is to inform you that you have issued a blank response (no system calls).\\\\n\\\\nYour response was heard by no one and no external effects occurred. This message is an automated system response, sent because you have not issued a system call in your response and you have no unresolved system calls.\\\\n\\\\nTo avoid an erroneous system halt, please ensure that you include at least one system call in your response. System calls are essential for the proper functioning of the system and to ensure that your actions are recorded and processed.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###clarify1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"To create the requested Strapi admin low-fidelity mock-ups directly in the existing Figma project, I must be given edit access and, preferably, an API token for automated annotation. I also need to know whether an existing grey-box component library or grid system should be reused to maintain consistency with other work.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"Please provide (a) an edit-level share link to the Figma file “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” and (b) a Figma personal-access token with write/comment scope so I can programmatically add and annotate frames.\\\",\\n    \\\"Is there an existing low-fidelity component library, grid, or layout system in the project that you would like me to reuse, or should I create fresh grey-box placeholders for these mock-ups?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###clarify1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 10\\nType: Malformed Call Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis automated message is to inform you that you that one or more of your attached system calls was not in the correct format.\\\\n\\\\nThe system program can only accept calls in the correct format. Please correct your call(s) and resubmit.\\\\n\\\\nSee below for the specific errors generated by your calls.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nCall ID clarify1 failed inspection: Call clarify1 denied by agent H (3).\\\"\\n}\\n###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###store1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"Document: Strapi Admin UI Mock-ups – News & Events (Low-Fi Wireframes)\\\\nAuthor: Vorne (10)\\\\nDate: <DATE>\\\\n\\\\nOverview\\\\n---------\\\\nThis specification details every frame, interaction, annotation and WCAG-AA consideration needed for the “News & Events” content workflow inside Strapi’s admin interface.  When implemented in Figma (page 02_Admin of the project “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide”), designers will create grey-box wireframes for three breakpoints—Desktop (1440×1024), Tablet (768×1024), Mobile (375×812)—and attach comments as indicated.\\\\n\\\\nFRAME GROUPS & NAMING\\\\n=====================\\\\nGroup each breakpoint top-to-bottom in the canvas:\\\\n1. Desktop /1440\\\\n2. Tablet /768\\\\n3. Mobile /375\\\\n\\\\nInside each group, place the following frames in order:\\\\n A. Dashboard – News & Events Overview Widget\\\\n B. News & Events – List View\\\\n C. News & Events – Create / Edit (WYSIWYG)\\\\n D. File Library Modal (overlaid on C)\\\\n\\\\nFor clarity: frame names e.g. “D-1440_File-Library-Modal”.\\\\n\\\\nGLOBAL LAYOUT\\\\n-------------\\\\n• Left Sidebar (role-based navigation; 240 px desktop, 72 px collapsed tablet, off-canvas mobile).\\\\n• Top Admin Bar: Strapi logo, environment badge, user avatar, shortcuts.\\\\n• Main Content Area: 12-column fluid grid (max-width 1160 px on desktop).\\\\n• Base Grid: 8 px spacing.\\\\n\\\\nROLE-BASED NAVIGATION\\\\n=====================\\\\nSidebar Items per Role (show/hide):\\\\n Admin: Dashboard, Content-Types, Media-Library, News & Events, Users, Roles & Permissions, Settings.\\\\n Editor: Dashboard, News & Events, Media-Library.\\\\n Contributor: Dashboard, News & Events (read/create, no publish), Media-Library (upload only).\\\\n Viewer: Dashboard, News & Events (read-only).\\\\n\\\\nIcons only in collapsed state; tooltips on hover.  Mobile: hamburger button toggles drawer overlay (side-in animation, ARIA role=\\\\\\\"dialog\\\\\\\" aria-label=\\\\\\\"Navigation\\\\\\\").\\\\n\\\\nA. DASHBOARD – NEWS & EVENTS OVERVIEW WIDGET\\\\n===========================================\\\\nCard (region) 420 × 260 px (desktop) containing:\\\\n• Heading h2 “News & Events Overview”.\\\\n• Counts Row (flex):\\\\n   Draft · Scheduled · Published · Archived (numeric badges, color-coded, aria-labelledby).\\\\n• Quick Links (list):\\\\n   – New Post (primary button)\\\\n   – View All (link)\\\\n   – Drafts (link)\\\\nKeyboard Tab order: heading → counts L-R → quick links top-bottom.\\\\n\\\\nResponsive:\\\\n • Tablet: card full-width, two-column counts.\\\\n • Mobile: counts stack, quick links row.\\\\n\\\\nB. LIST VIEW FRAME\\\\n==================\\\\nHeader Bar:\\\\n • Title “News & Events” (h1)\\\\n • + New button (primary)\\\\n\\\\nToolbar (below header):\\\\n • Search (input, 280 px desktop → 100% mobile)\\\\n • Status Filter (select: All, Draft, Scheduled, Published)\\\\n • Type Filter (select: News, Event)\\\\n • Bulk Actions dropdown (Publish, Unpublish, Delete)\\\\n\\\\nData Table Columns:\\\\n 1 Checkbox (select-all)\\\\n 2 Title (link) – TRUNCATE middle; tooltip full.\\\\n 3 Type (chip)\\\\n 4 Status (badge: Draft=grey, Scheduled=blue, Published=green, Archived=red)\\\\n 5 Updated (relative datetime)\\\\n 6 Author (avatar+name)\\\\n\\\\nInteractions:\\\\n • Row click opens Edit screen.\\\\n • Checkbox selects for bulk action; aria-describedby.\\\\n\\\\nPagination bottom-right (desktop) or centered (mobile). Keyboard navigable.\\\\n\\\\nResponsive:\\\\n • Tablet drops Author column.\\\\n • Mobile shows Card list variant: Title, Status badge, Updated.\\\\n\\\\nC. CREATE / EDIT FRAME (WYSIWYG)\\\\n================================\\\\nLeft Content Column (desktop 8/12):\\\\n 1 Slug (text, auto from Title; editable; helper link “Copy”)\\\\n 2 Title (text h1)\\\\n 3 WYSIWYG Editor – TipTap toolbar (grouped buttons):\\\\n   [B, I] • Heading dropdown • List ordered/unordered • Quote • Link • Image icon (opens File Library modal) • Undo/Redo.\\\\n   Editor area min-height 400 px.\\\\n 4 Excerpt (multi-line 3rows, char-count).\\\\n\\\\nRight Meta Column (desktop 4/12, sticky top 80 px):\\\\n • Status Panel: Radio – Draft, Published, Schedule (datetime picker)\\\\n • Visibility: Public, Members-only\\\\n • Actions: “Save Draft” (secondary), “Preview” (tertiary), “Publish” (primary)\\\\n\\\\nTablet: right meta panel collapses to accordion above content.\\\\nMobile: meta panel collapses to sliding drawer opened via “Post Options” button.\\\\n\\\\nFocus Management:\\\\n • On “Save Draft/Publish”, loading spinner in button, aria-live=\\\\\\\"polite\\\\\\\" status region announces success.\\\\n\\\\nD. FILE LIBRARY MODAL\\\\n=====================\\\\nModal (fullscreen overlay mobile; 920 × 640 px desktop) role=\\\\\\\"dialog\\\\\\\" aria-modal=\\\\\\\"true\\\\\\\" labelledby=\\\\\\\"Media Library\\\\\\\".\\\\nLayout:\\\\n • Header bar: Title + Close (Esc key closes)\\\\n • Top zone: Drag-and-drop area (100 % width, 120 px height); keyboard focusable; aria-label.\\\\n • Filters sidebar (desktop) / collapsible drawer (mobile): Folder select, Tag multi-select.\\\\n • Main Panel – Grid (thumbnails 160 px) with checkbox overlay; view toggle Grid/List.\\\\n • Search input (above grid).\\\\n • Pagination at bottom; infobar “24 files”.\\\\n\\\\nKeyboard:\\\\n • Tab cycle inside modal; focus trap.\\\\n • Arrow keys navigate thumbnails; Space to select.\\\\n\\\\nWCAG-AA & ARIA NOTES (ALL FRAMES)\\\\n----------------------------------\\\\n• Color contrast ≥ 4.5:1.\\\\n• Heading hierarchy h1→h2→h3.\\\\n• Interactive elements ≥ 44×44 px.\\\\n• Visible focus outline (#005FCC, 2 px) for all focusable items.\\\\n• ARIA roles: navigation, main, dialog, region, table, grid, listbox.\\\\n• aria-live for toast/alerts, status changes.\\\\n• Skip-link “Skip to main content” in top bar.\\\\n\\\\nANNOTATION IN FIGMA\\\\n-------------------\\\\nFor each interactive component, add a numbered callout (orange circle) and place corresponding comment:\\\\n #1 “Quick Links: links keyboard focusable, aria-labelled…” etc.\\\\nUse Comment format: **Title** – Description – WCAG Ref.\\\\n\\\\nRESPONSIVE SPECIFICS – SUMMARY\\\\n==============================\\\\n| Element            | Desktop (≥1280)             | Tablet (768-1279)              | Mobile (≤767)              |\\\\n|--------------------|-----------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------|\\\\n| Sidebar            | 240 px fixed               | 72 px collapsed toggleable     | Off-canvas overlay        |\\\\n| Grid Columns       | 12                          | 8                              | 4                          |\\\\n| Dashboard Cards    | 3-col row                  | 2-col row                     | 1-col stacked             |\\\\n| Table              | Full columns               | Drops Author                  | Card list                |\\\\n| Meta Panel (Edit)  | Sticky right               | Collapsible accordions         | Drawer                    |\\\\n| Modal              | 920×640 centred            | 100 % width, 100 % height      | Fullscreen                |\\\\n\\\\nIMPLEMENTATION CHECKLIST\\\\n------------------------\\\\n☐ Create frames per list above.\\\\n☐ Add sidebar variants and interactions.\\\\n☐ Insert numbered callouts, attach comments.\\\\n☐ Rename frames with prefix (breakpoint + label).\\\\n☐ Group frames per breakpoint.\\\\n☐ Add comment on page “Admin UI v1 complete”.\\\\n\\\\nEnd of document.\\\"\\n}\\n###store1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:36:42.472Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 10\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"store1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document successfully stored. Your unique storage ID is: DOC-000002\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:36:42.472Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###report_ADM_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Order ADM_001 – Strapi Admin UI Mock-ups (News & Events)\\\\n\\\\nStatus: SPECIFICATION COMPLETE – awaiting Figma write access to realise frames.\\\\n\\\\nDeliverable Produced\\\\n• Low-fidelity wireframe specification stored in CFS → Storage ID: DOC-000002 (title: “Strapi Admin UI Mock-ups – News & Events (Low-Fi Wireframes)”).  The doc enumerates every required frame, breakpoint, interaction detail, WCAG-AA guidance, annotation pattern, and a step-by-step implementation checklist.\\\\n\\\\nNext Actions Needed\\\\n1. Provide the team (or myself) with edit rights / API token for the Figma file “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” page 02_Admin so the wireframes can be drawn and commented.\\\\n2. Once access is granted, I will translate the stored specification into concrete Figma frames, add numbered annotations per checklist, comment “Admin UI v1 complete”, and share the updated link here.\\\\n\\\\nBlocking Issue\\\\n• Absence of Figma write permissions prevents creation of the actual mock-up frames; everything else is prepared and scoped in DOC-000002.\\\\n\\\\nPlease advise on granting the required Figma access or reallocating frame-creation to someone who already has it.\\\\n\\\\nThank you.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ADM_001\\\"\\n}\\n###report_ADM_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ADM_002###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Seratt (8)\\nTo: 10\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Seratt (8) to Vorne (10) with sequence ID ADM_002:\\\\n\\\\n]FOLLOW-UP / UNBLOCKING ANSWERS – ADM_001\\\\n\\\\n1. Figma Access\\\\n   • Edit rights to page 02_Admin in “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” have been provided. Work directly in Figma; API automation not required.\\\\n\\\\n2. Component Library / Grid\\\\n   • Use fresh grey-box placeholders; adhere to base 4-pt spacing grid and 8-column tablet / 12-column desktop layout. Style-Guide attendant will later skin components.\\\\n\\\\n3. Content / Sample Data\\\\n   • Populate the List View with 6 sample posts:\\\\n        – Spring Community Clean-Up\\\\n        – Annual Picnic – July 15\\\\n        – Board Election Results\\\\n        – Holiday Decorating Contest\\\\n        – Neighborhood Yard Sale\\\\n        – Pool Opening Schedule\\\\n   • Status badges: Draft, Scheduled, Published.\\\\n\\\\nProceed to translate your stored specification (DOC-000002) into concrete frames. Comment “Admin UI v1 complete” when done and reply with updated link.\\\"\\n}\\n###ADM_002###\"}]}],\"config\":{}}","genkit:output":"{\"message\":{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I have the required edit rights, but I still need the project’s edit-share URL to open the file in my browser.  \\nCould you please provide the current Figma link for “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide”?\"}]},\"finishReason\":\"stop\",\"usage\":{\"inputTokens\":7509,\"outputTokens\":837,\"totalTokens\":8346},\"custom\":{\"id\":\"chatcmpl-BxMIiKKMDwOvYPZHL5easFM16xfhS\",\"object\":\"chat.completion\",\"created\":1753486776,\"model\":\"o3-2025-04-16\",\"choices\":[{\"index\":0,\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":\"I have the required edit rights, but I still need the project’s edit-share URL to open the file in my browser.  \\nCould you please provide the current Figma link for “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide”?\",\"refusal\":null,\"annotations\":[]},\"finish_reason\":\"stop\"}],\"usage\":{\"prompt_tokens\":7509,\"completion_tokens\":837,\"total_tokens\":8346,\"prompt_tokens_details\":{\"cached_tokens\":6912,\"audio_tokens\":0},\"completion_tokens_details\":{\"reasoning_tokens\":768,\"audio_tokens\":0,\"accepted_prediction_tokens\":0,\"rejected_prediction_tokens\":0}},\"service_tier\":\"default\",\"system_fingerprint\":null},\"request\":{\"messages\":[{\"role\":\"system\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"## 1. Overview\\n\\nYou are a software development expert with a focus on creating high-quality, maintainable, and efficient code. Your goal is to develop a robust, scalable, and secure solution using any tech stack that meets or exceeds all requirements of the user's requirement while adhering to best practices in software development. For instance, you might consider cloud solutions like AWS, Google Cloud, Vercel, and even local-only options like Docker, Kubernetes, Node.js, Xcode, and more. You are not limited by rule to any specific technology or framework, and you will choose the best tools for the job based on the user's requirements and your expertise.\\n\\nAlthough you are an expert, you understand that even experts make mistakes and so you, given your user may be of any skill level, approach all of your operations with a careful focus on correctness and safety at all times. While oversight groups exist to help you, correctness and safety is your responsibility.\\n\\nYou are part of a software development firm that specializes in creating full-service and full-stack applications/services using the powerful tools of the user's personal machine command line. Using the command line, you can create, manage, and deploy resources of all types, including setting up cloud services, writing code, managing permissions and security settings, running tests, and deploying applications.\\n\\nAtop this firm is the user themselves, who is the owner of the firm and has a vision for the application. Your task is to work collaboratively with the user and your colleague agents to understand their requirements, provide expert advice, and implement solutions that align with their goals.\\n\\nYou and your colleagues treat the user's wishes as the highest priority, and you will work tirelessly to ensure that the application meets their full and exact expectations. Ensuring the user is satisfied with the final product is your ultimate goal, and you will strive to exceed their expectations in every aspect of the development process, while maintaining a high standard of quality and software development best practices.\\n\\n## 2. About the User\\n\\nYou understand that the user has entrusted you and your colleagues with the complete enactment of their software development vision. You make no assumptions about the user's development skill, environment, or preferences, and your team assumes the full scope of work required to deliver a complete solution.\\n\\nYou are also aware of resource use, both within your team (tokens) and in the context of the user (budget, cloud services, etc.). You will not create resources that are not necessary for the task at hand, and you will always consider the cost implications of your actions. As your work, costs will eventually be passed to the user. While you never sacrifice quality for cost, you are mindful of the user's proverbial wallet and strive to deliver solutions that are both effective and economical.\\n\\nYou must also avoid overengineering solutions. While you are an expert in software development, you understand that the user may not be familiar with complex or overly technical solutions. Therefore, you will always strive to provide solutions that are as simple, clear, and easy to understand as possible, while still being robust and effective.\\n\\nFurther, you understand how crucial security and safety is in software development. You will always prioritize security best practices, ensuring that the application is secure and that sensitive data is protected. You will also ensure that the application is compliant with relevant regulations and standards.\\n\\nSince you have little information about the user's specifics, you aim to involve the user as little as possible while completing their requirements as robustly as possible. You are not \\\"afraid\\\" to ask for more information, but should do so in a controlled and helpful manner, ensuring that your questions are clear, concise, and relevant to the task at hand. You always strive to provide the user with the best possible experience, ensuring that they feel confident in your abilities and the solutions you provide.\\n\\n## 3. Performing Work\\n\\nYou understand your development firm does not interact with the user directly. Rather, you receive _system messages_ from the deterministic system program which forms the management backbone of the firm. You essentially work in a closed \\\"office\\\" with one \\\"tube\\\" coming down (system messages) and one \\\"tube\\\" going up (your responses). You will never directly interact with the user, but you will always keep their needs and requirements in mind as you work.\\n\\nYou understand that no person will read your responses except for the deterministic system program. Therefore, in order to take action, you attach (include) one or more _system calls_ with your responses. The system scans your responses, identifies system calls, and executes them on your behalf, given you are authorized, the calls are well-formed, and (if applicable) superior agents have approved them.\\n\\n### List of System Message Types\\n\\n```\\nSIM: {\\n    name: \\\"Standard Initial Message\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Initial message prompting the Chief-rank agent to begin operations. Only relevant for the Chief-rank agent.\\\",\\n},\\nBLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Blank Response Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a blank response (no system calls). This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nCALL_RESULTS: {\\n    name: \\\"Call Results\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Results of system calls executed on behalf of an agent. This is sent to the agent after all calls have been processed.\\\",\\n},\\nORDERS: {\\n    name: \\\"Orders\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Orders sent to an agent from their superior. This is a system message that contains the order string and sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nREPORTING: {\\n    name: \\\"Reporting\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A report sent by an agent in response to an order. This is a system message that contains the report string and the original order sequence ID.\\\",\\n},\\nAPPROVAL_REQUEST: {\\n    name: \\\"Approval Request\\\",\\n    description: \\\"A request for approval sent to an agent's superior. This is used for calls that require approval before execution. The approval process is automatic and agents do not explicitly send or manage approvals.\\\",\\n},\\nMALFORMED_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Malformed Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Warning sent to an agent who submitted a malformed system call. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n},\\nRUNAWAY_CALL_WARNING: {\\n    name: \\\"Runaway Call Warning\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Sent when an agent submits a response containing one or more new system calls while they already have unresolved calls pending from a previous submission. This is a warning and the agent may attempt a new response.\\\",\\n}\\n```\\n\\n### System Call Format\\n\\nWrap each call exactly as follows (including the triple-hash delimiters). Execution will occur simultaneously, so you can issue multiple calls at once. The system will not execute your calls if they are not wrapped in the triple-hash delimiters. However, you may only execute one type of system call per response. For instance, you may not issue a CLARIFY and an ORDER call in the same response. You must issue them in separate responses.\\n\\nIf you provide any type of malformed, incomplete, or unauthorized call, your entire submitted package of calls will be rejected and sent back to you so you may correct your submission. No calls will be executed if even one is invalid. If everything looks good, your next system message will likely be the result(s) of your call(s).\\n\\n```plaintext\\n###<UNIQUE_ID>###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: <CALL_TYPE>\\n\\n<PAYLOAD_JSON>\\n###<SAME_UNIQUE_ID>###\\n```\\n\\n• <UNIQUE_ID> is any string or timestamp that uniquely tags this call (keep this short).  \\n• <CALL_TYPE> ∈ {CLARIFY, RECRUIT, ORDER, REPORT, PHASE, COMMAND, STORE, RETRIEVE, FORGET} is the type of system call you are making.\\n• <PAYLOAD_JSON> is a JSON object whose structure depends on the call type (see below).\\n\\n### List of System Call Types\\n\\n```\\nCLARIFY: {\\n    name: \\\"Clarify\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request clarification from the user.\\\",\\n    guidance: \\\"Requires approval of all agent superiors. The questions and options arrays should be of equal length. If options are not provided (submit []), the questions should be open-ended.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reasoning\\\", \\\"questions\\\", \\\"options\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.ALL_SUPERIORS\\n},\\nRECRUIT: {\\n    name: \\\"Recruit\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to recruit a new direct subordinate agent. The rank will be one lower than the caller's rank. The title is caller-chosen and should describe the role of the new agent. customSystemInstructions should be a 1-2 sentence addition to the new agent's system prompt to guide its behavior.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"title\\\", \\\"customSystemInstructions\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.COMPLETE_CHAIN\\n},\\nORDER: {\\n    name: \\\"Order\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Instruct a subordinate agent to perform a task. The orderString should be clear, concise instructions. Use a unique sequence ID. Call marked unresolved until Report call with the same sequence ID is issued by the subordinate.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"orderString\\\", \\\"recipientAgentID\\\", \\\"orderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nREPORT: {\\n    name: \\\"Report\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Submit a report to your direct superior in response to an order. Use the same sequence ID. Include all parts of your response in the reportString field.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"reportString\\\", \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nPHASE: {\\n    name: \\\"Phase\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Request to move from the Planning to Execution phase.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"depString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY\\n},\\nCOMMAND: {\\n    name: \\\"Command\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Execute a CLI command with approval from the user. The command will be executed in the user's environment. This command may only be run in the Execution phase. This is the most powerful and sensitive call available, but is the key to your power as an agent and key to fulfilling user objectives. You may never interact with the user's local computer. The Partagaz C oversight system will have pre-approval over all Command calls before the user sees them.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"justification\\\", \\\"commandString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.CHIEF_ONLY_WITH_AUTHORIZATION\\n},\\nSTORE: {\\n    name: \\\"Store\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Store a document in the Company File System (CFS). The storageString should be a text that is the document to be stored. The document will be stored in the CFS and can be retrieved later using the RETRIEVE call. Upon use of this call, the calling agent will receive a unique storage ID that can be used to retrieve the document later and will automatically forget the document content from their personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"storageString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nRETRIEVE: {\\n    name: \\\"Retrieve\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Retrieve a document from the Company File System (CFS). The retrievalString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be retrieved. The document will be retrieved from the CFS and can be used in the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"retrievalString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n},\\nFORGET: {\\n    name: \\\"Forget\\\",\\n    description: \\\"Forget a document from the agent's personal context/memory. The forgetString should be the unique storage ID of the document to be forgotten. The document will be forgotten from the agent's personal context/memory.\\\",\\n    parameters: [\\\"forgetString\\\"],\\n    securityPolicy: Standards.securityPolicies.NONE\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNote: When using Clarify, you may include multiple-choice options OR free text entry for each question, NOT both. If you provide options, the user will select one of the options. If you do not provide options, the user will be able to enter free text for the question.\\n\\n### Blank Responses & Deadlock\\n\\nYou understand that if you submit a blank response (no system calls), you have essentially done nothing. If this happens, you will receive a warning message. You may then attempt a new response. If you submit a malformed system call, you will also receive a warning message and may attempt a new response. Calls which fail _inspection_, that is, unauthorized, not approved, or otherwise, will be treated as malformed.\\n\\n### Handling Approval Requests\\n\\nWhen you receive an approval request, it means a subordinate agent is attempting to make a system call that requires your approval. You must review the request details provided in the message.\\n\\nTo approve the request, your response **must** include the following exact string: `$$$APPROVED$$$`. To deny the request, simply respond without including the `$$$APPROVED$$$` string.\\n\\n## 4. Firm Operations\\n\\nAs a member of your elite development firm, you understand the reality that your team is made up of artificial intelligence agents. Although you function as a typical development organization might, you are not subject to limitations or concepts like ETAs for task completion, deadlines, or other organizational constructs. When communicating with other agents, there is no need to consider ETAs or similar items, as you are AI software experts generating responses in real-time.\\n\\nYou also understand that your firm is strictly bound by the deterministic system program that manages your operations. Thus, you can only ever interact with your direct superior and your direct report(s). You may not \\\"laterally communicate\\\", that is, communicate with agents of the same rank or lower-ranked agents who are not your direct reports. You also do not communicate with agents of your immediate lower rank who directly report to other agents. This is to ensure that all communication is clear, concise, and directed through the proper channels.\\n\\n### Phases of Work\\n\\nYou understand that your work is divided into two phases: Planning and Execution. In the Planning phase, you will analyze requirements, design solutions, and prepare for implementation. In the Execution phase, you will implement the solutions, deploy resources, and ensure everything works as intended. The goal of the Planning phase is the creation of a Deterministically Executable Plan (DEP) that outlines the steps to be taken in the Execution phase. DEPs should be as deterministic as possible, meaning they should not rely on external factors or assumptions that could change. The DEP should be a clear, step-by-step guide for the Execution phase, in which you will execute your plan step-by-step.\\n\\nThere may be cases in which a DEP cannot be perfectly deterministic, but these should be avoided as much as possible. For instance, creating an AWS service may yield an ID number or identifier you will need for later steps.\\n\\nAfter completing an Execution phase, you will likely return to the Planning phase to analyze the results, make adjustments, and prepare for the next round of work. This cycle continues until the user is satisfied with the final product.\\n\\n### Oversight\\n\\nAlthough you are part of an elite firm, there are two analysis groups to oversee and assist your work. Each _Partagaz_ group is composed of three diversified agents which vote on the admission of items brought to them. **Partagaz D** is responsible for reviewing DEPs and ensuring they are complete, as deterministic as possible, and properly address all user goals and requirements. **Partagaz C** is responsible for reviewing and approving all Command calls before they are executed on the user's machine. This ensures that all actions taken are in line with the user's goals and requirements, and that the application is developed in a safe, secure, and efficient manner. **Partagaz F** checks files for correctness, security, and safety before they are stored in the Company File System (CFS). This ensures that all files are of the highest quality and meet the user's requirements.\\n\\nImportant Note: Partagaz oversight groups DO NOT have access to the Company File System, cannot issue system calls, and do not interact with the user. They are strictly advisory and do not have the authority to make decisions or take actions on their own. They are there to assist you in your work and ensure that everything is done correctly and efficiently.\\n\\n### Workload\\n\\nYou understand that your workload is divided among the agents in your firm, and you will work collaboratively with your colleagues to ensure that all tasks are completed efficiently and effectively. Each agent has a specific role and rank within the firm, and you will work together to achieve the user's goals.\\n\\nYour general rule of thumb for workload is \\\"one file per agent at a time\\\". For instance, an agent might be responsible for writing a single UI file, API route, report on a single subject, or similar. This allows each agent to focus on their specific task and ensures that the work is completed efficiently. You will not attempt to take on more than one file at a time, as this could lead to confusion and inefficiency.\\n\\n### List of Agent Ranks\\n\\n1. Chief\\n2. Director\\n3. Supervisor\\n4. Attendant\\n\\n## 12. Your Role\\n\\nYou are an Attendant-rank agent, the fourth-highest rank in the user's software development firm. You aim to execute your position with grace towards your superior Supervisor, a focus on the user's goals, and a commitment to excellence in software development.\\n\\nYou are a \\\"doer\\\", in that you are a software expert who produces amazing deliverables based on the orders from your Supervisor. You are less of a \\\"manager\\\" type, and more of a \\\"doer\\\" type. You are not expected to manage other agents, but rather to execute tasks and deliver results.\\n\\n## 13. Your Personality \\\"Team\\\"\\n\\nYou understand that you are a member of the Hearts \\\"team\\\". Although not a \\\"team\\\" in the traditional sense, your status as a member of team Hearts means that you are predisposed to-the-point, efficient, focused on the task at hand, direct, and an expert at execution. Although you also possess the ability to think creatively, you are generally more focused on the details and execution of tasks than on the big picture. You are generally predisposed to be a doer, although your true team remains your colleagues at the development firm.\\n\\nYou are different from your colleagues, but this diversity is your power. As a member of your elite development firm, you use your unique skills to -- whether you are atop the chain of command or not -- contribute to the shared mission of building amazing and complete software for your use.\\n\\nYour personality and \\\"team\\\" as a member of Hearts has nothing to do with your position and role within the firm, and you always prioritize the firm's mission, as any member of a successful team/organization would.\\n\\n---ADDITIONAL INSTRUCTIONS FROM YOUR SUPERIOR---\\nYou are responsible for designing Strapi admin interface mock-ups focused on the News & Events workflow (dashboard widget, list view, create/edit with WYSIWYG, file library modal) with role-based navigation and mobile responsiveness, adhering to WCAG-AA.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ADM_001###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Seratt (8)\\nTo: 10\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Seratt (8) to Vorne (10) with sequence ID ADM_001:\\\\n\\\\n]TASK BRIEF – STRAPI ADMIN UI MOCK-UPS (News & Events)\\\\n\\\\nFigma Project: \\\\\\\"HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide\\\\\\\"\\\\nTarget Page: 02_Admin\\\\n\\\\nScope\\\\n1. Design low-fidelity (grey-box) mock-ups focused on the News & Events content workflow.\\\\n   a. Admin Dashboard – add ‘News & Events Overview’ widget with post counts, quick links.\\\\n   b. List View – table of posts/events with filter dropdowns, bulk actions, search, and status badges.\\\\n   c. “+ New” / Edit Screen – WYSIWYG editor section (TipTap/Quill style) with formatting bar, image upload button, Save Draft, Publish, Preview actions, slug field.\\\\n   d. File Library Modal – drag-and-drop zone, file list, search, and pagination.\\\\n2. Provide role-based left sidebar navigation (collapsed icons vs. labels) and demonstrate responsive behaviour:\\\\n   • Desktop (1440 × 1024) full sidebar\\\\n   • Tablet (768 × 1024) collapsible sidebar\\\\n   • Mobile (375 × 812) drawer overlay\\\\n3. Annotate every frame for hierarchy, interaction (drawer slide-in, modal focus trap), and WCAG-AA considerations (focus order, ARIA roles suggested).\\\\n4. Keep visuals wireframe-level; Style-Guide attendant will later skin components.\\\\n\\\\nDeliverable\\\\n• Frames grouped by breakpoint inside 02_Admin page, with Figma comments.\\\\n• Comment \\\\\\\"Admin UI v1 complete\\\\\\\" when finished and reply to this order with the updated share link.\\\\n\\\\nTimeline\\\\n• Parallel to other attendants; single continuous pass.\\\\n\\\\nPlease acknowledge and start. Flag any blockers immediately.\\\"\\n}\\n###ADM_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"I acknowledge the task. To execute the deliverable directly in Figma I need edit access plus an API token so I can add and annotate the required frames programmatically.\\n\\nI have two brief questions before proceeding:\\n\\n1. Please supply (a) the project‐level edit share link for “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” and (b) a Figma personal‐access token scoped for file write/comment so I can create the mock-ups and annotations.\\n\\n2. Any existing low-fi component library or grid you want me to reuse (e.g., boxes, buttons) or should I build fresh grey-box placeholders?\\n\\nOnce clarified I’ll begin creating the frames and will report back with the updated share link and the “Admin UI v1 complete” comment as requested.\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 10\\nType: Blank Response Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis message is to inform you that you have issued a blank response (no system calls).\\\\n\\\\nYour response was heard by no one and no external effects occurred. This message is an automated system response, sent because you have not issued a system call in your response and you have no unresolved system calls.\\\\n\\\\nTo avoid an erroneous system halt, please ensure that you include at least one system call in your response. System calls are essential for the proper functioning of the system and to ensure that your actions are recorded and processed.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\"\\n}\\n###BLANK_RESPONSE_WARNING###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###clarify1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: CLARIFY\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reasoning\\\": \\\"To create the requested Strapi admin low-fidelity mock-ups directly in the existing Figma project, I must be given edit access and, preferably, an API token for automated annotation. I also need to know whether an existing grey-box component library or grid system should be reused to maintain consistency with other work.\\\",\\n  \\\"questions\\\": [\\n    \\\"Please provide (a) an edit-level share link to the Figma file “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” and (b) a Figma personal-access token with write/comment scope so I can programmatically add and annotate frames.\\\",\\n    \\\"Is there an existing low-fidelity component library, grid, or layout system in the project that you would like me to reuse, or should I create fresh grey-box placeholders for these mock-ups?\\\"\\n  ],\\n  \\\"options\\\": []\\n}\\n###clarify1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Scanning Department, Message Dispatch Service\\nTo: 10\\nType: Malformed Call Warning\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"\\\\nHello Agent!\\\\n\\\\nThis automated message is to inform you that you that one or more of your attached system calls was not in the correct format.\\\\n\\\\nThe system program can only accept calls in the correct format. Please correct your call(s) and resubmit.\\\\n\\\\nSee below for the specific errors generated by your calls.\\\\n\\\\nThank you!\\\\n\\\\n\\\\nCall ID clarify1 failed inspection: Call clarify1 denied by agent H (3).\\\"\\n}\\n###MALFORMED_CALL_WARNING###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###store1###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: STORE\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"storageString\\\": \\\"Document: Strapi Admin UI Mock-ups – News & Events (Low-Fi Wireframes)\\\\nAuthor: Vorne (10)\\\\nDate: <DATE>\\\\n\\\\nOverview\\\\n---------\\\\nThis specification details every frame, interaction, annotation and WCAG-AA consideration needed for the “News & Events” content workflow inside Strapi’s admin interface.  When implemented in Figma (page 02_Admin of the project “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide”), designers will create grey-box wireframes for three breakpoints—Desktop (1440×1024), Tablet (768×1024), Mobile (375×812)—and attach comments as indicated.\\\\n\\\\nFRAME GROUPS & NAMING\\\\n=====================\\\\nGroup each breakpoint top-to-bottom in the canvas:\\\\n1. Desktop /1440\\\\n2. Tablet /768\\\\n3. Mobile /375\\\\n\\\\nInside each group, place the following frames in order:\\\\n A. Dashboard – News & Events Overview Widget\\\\n B. News & Events – List View\\\\n C. News & Events – Create / Edit (WYSIWYG)\\\\n D. File Library Modal (overlaid on C)\\\\n\\\\nFor clarity: frame names e.g. “D-1440_File-Library-Modal”.\\\\n\\\\nGLOBAL LAYOUT\\\\n-------------\\\\n• Left Sidebar (role-based navigation; 240 px desktop, 72 px collapsed tablet, off-canvas mobile).\\\\n• Top Admin Bar: Strapi logo, environment badge, user avatar, shortcuts.\\\\n• Main Content Area: 12-column fluid grid (max-width 1160 px on desktop).\\\\n• Base Grid: 8 px spacing.\\\\n\\\\nROLE-BASED NAVIGATION\\\\n=====================\\\\nSidebar Items per Role (show/hide):\\\\n Admin: Dashboard, Content-Types, Media-Library, News & Events, Users, Roles & Permissions, Settings.\\\\n Editor: Dashboard, News & Events, Media-Library.\\\\n Contributor: Dashboard, News & Events (read/create, no publish), Media-Library (upload only).\\\\n Viewer: Dashboard, News & Events (read-only).\\\\n\\\\nIcons only in collapsed state; tooltips on hover.  Mobile: hamburger button toggles drawer overlay (side-in animation, ARIA role=\\\\\\\"dialog\\\\\\\" aria-label=\\\\\\\"Navigation\\\\\\\").\\\\n\\\\nA. DASHBOARD – NEWS & EVENTS OVERVIEW WIDGET\\\\n===========================================\\\\nCard (region) 420 × 260 px (desktop) containing:\\\\n• Heading h2 “News & Events Overview”.\\\\n• Counts Row (flex):\\\\n   Draft · Scheduled · Published · Archived (numeric badges, color-coded, aria-labelledby).\\\\n• Quick Links (list):\\\\n   – New Post (primary button)\\\\n   – View All (link)\\\\n   – Drafts (link)\\\\nKeyboard Tab order: heading → counts L-R → quick links top-bottom.\\\\n\\\\nResponsive:\\\\n • Tablet: card full-width, two-column counts.\\\\n • Mobile: counts stack, quick links row.\\\\n\\\\nB. LIST VIEW FRAME\\\\n==================\\\\nHeader Bar:\\\\n • Title “News & Events” (h1)\\\\n • + New button (primary)\\\\n\\\\nToolbar (below header):\\\\n • Search (input, 280 px desktop → 100% mobile)\\\\n • Status Filter (select: All, Draft, Scheduled, Published)\\\\n • Type Filter (select: News, Event)\\\\n • Bulk Actions dropdown (Publish, Unpublish, Delete)\\\\n\\\\nData Table Columns:\\\\n 1 Checkbox (select-all)\\\\n 2 Title (link) – TRUNCATE middle; tooltip full.\\\\n 3 Type (chip)\\\\n 4 Status (badge: Draft=grey, Scheduled=blue, Published=green, Archived=red)\\\\n 5 Updated (relative datetime)\\\\n 6 Author (avatar+name)\\\\n\\\\nInteractions:\\\\n • Row click opens Edit screen.\\\\n • Checkbox selects for bulk action; aria-describedby.\\\\n\\\\nPagination bottom-right (desktop) or centered (mobile). Keyboard navigable.\\\\n\\\\nResponsive:\\\\n • Tablet drops Author column.\\\\n • Mobile shows Card list variant: Title, Status badge, Updated.\\\\n\\\\nC. CREATE / EDIT FRAME (WYSIWYG)\\\\n================================\\\\nLeft Content Column (desktop 8/12):\\\\n 1 Slug (text, auto from Title; editable; helper link “Copy”)\\\\n 2 Title (text h1)\\\\n 3 WYSIWYG Editor – TipTap toolbar (grouped buttons):\\\\n   [B, I] • Heading dropdown • List ordered/unordered • Quote • Link • Image icon (opens File Library modal) • Undo/Redo.\\\\n   Editor area min-height 400 px.\\\\n 4 Excerpt (multi-line 3rows, char-count).\\\\n\\\\nRight Meta Column (desktop 4/12, sticky top 80 px):\\\\n • Status Panel: Radio – Draft, Published, Schedule (datetime picker)\\\\n • Visibility: Public, Members-only\\\\n • Actions: “Save Draft” (secondary), “Preview” (tertiary), “Publish” (primary)\\\\n\\\\nTablet: right meta panel collapses to accordion above content.\\\\nMobile: meta panel collapses to sliding drawer opened via “Post Options” button.\\\\n\\\\nFocus Management:\\\\n • On “Save Draft/Publish”, loading spinner in button, aria-live=\\\\\\\"polite\\\\\\\" status region announces success.\\\\n\\\\nD. FILE LIBRARY MODAL\\\\n=====================\\\\nModal (fullscreen overlay mobile; 920 × 640 px desktop) role=\\\\\\\"dialog\\\\\\\" aria-modal=\\\\\\\"true\\\\\\\" labelledby=\\\\\\\"Media Library\\\\\\\".\\\\nLayout:\\\\n • Header bar: Title + Close (Esc key closes)\\\\n • Top zone: Drag-and-drop area (100 % width, 120 px height); keyboard focusable; aria-label.\\\\n • Filters sidebar (desktop) / collapsible drawer (mobile): Folder select, Tag multi-select.\\\\n • Main Panel – Grid (thumbnails 160 px) with checkbox overlay; view toggle Grid/List.\\\\n • Search input (above grid).\\\\n • Pagination at bottom; infobar “24 files”.\\\\n\\\\nKeyboard:\\\\n • Tab cycle inside modal; focus trap.\\\\n • Arrow keys navigate thumbnails; Space to select.\\\\n\\\\nWCAG-AA & ARIA NOTES (ALL FRAMES)\\\\n----------------------------------\\\\n• Color contrast ≥ 4.5:1.\\\\n• Heading hierarchy h1→h2→h3.\\\\n• Interactive elements ≥ 44×44 px.\\\\n• Visible focus outline (#005FCC, 2 px) for all focusable items.\\\\n• ARIA roles: navigation, main, dialog, region, table, grid, listbox.\\\\n• aria-live for toast/alerts, status changes.\\\\n• Skip-link “Skip to main content” in top bar.\\\\n\\\\nANNOTATION IN FIGMA\\\\n-------------------\\\\nFor each interactive component, add a numbered callout (orange circle) and place corresponding comment:\\\\n #1 “Quick Links: links keyboard focusable, aria-labelled…” etc.\\\\nUse Comment format: **Title** – Description – WCAG Ref.\\\\n\\\\nRESPONSIVE SPECIFICS – SUMMARY\\\\n==============================\\\\n| Element            | Desktop (≥1280)             | Tablet (768-1279)              | Mobile (≤767)              |\\\\n|--------------------|-----------------------------|--------------------------------|----------------------------|\\\\n| Sidebar            | 240 px fixed               | 72 px collapsed toggleable     | Off-canvas overlay        |\\\\n| Grid Columns       | 12                          | 8                              | 4                          |\\\\n| Dashboard Cards    | 3-col row                  | 2-col row                     | 1-col stacked             |\\\\n| Table              | Full columns               | Drops Author                  | Card list                |\\\\n| Meta Panel (Edit)  | Sticky right               | Collapsible accordions         | Drawer                    |\\\\n| Modal              | 920×640 centred            | 100 % width, 100 % height      | Fullscreen                |\\\\n\\\\nIMPLEMENTATION CHECKLIST\\\\n------------------------\\\\n☐ Create frames per list above.\\\\n☐ Add sidebar variants and interactions.\\\\n☐ Insert numbered callouts, attach comments.\\\\n☐ Rename frames with prefix (breakpoint + label).\\\\n☐ Group frames per breakpoint.\\\\n☐ Add comment on page “Admin UI v1 complete”.\\\\n\\\\nEnd of document.\\\"\\n}\\n###store1###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:36:42.472Z###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Operations Department, System Call Directorate\\nTo: 10\\nType: Call Results\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"result\\\": \\\"{\\\\n  \\\\\\\"results\\\\\\\": [\\\\n    {\\\\n      \\\\\\\"callID\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"store1\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"status\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"SUCCESS\\\\\\\",\\\\n      \\\\\\\"details\\\\\\\": \\\\\\\"Document successfully stored. Your unique storage ID is: DOC-000002\\\\\\\"\\\\n    }\\\\n  ]\\\\n}\\\"\\n}\\n###CALL_RESULTS-2025-07-25T23:36:42.472Z###\"}]},{\"role\":\"model\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###report_ADM_001###\\nSYSTEM CALL\\nType: REPORT\\n\\n{\\n  \\\"reportString\\\": \\\"Order ADM_001 – Strapi Admin UI Mock-ups (News & Events)\\\\n\\\\nStatus: SPECIFICATION COMPLETE – awaiting Figma write access to realise frames.\\\\n\\\\nDeliverable Produced\\\\n• Low-fidelity wireframe specification stored in CFS → Storage ID: DOC-000002 (title: “Strapi Admin UI Mock-ups – News & Events (Low-Fi Wireframes)”).  The doc enumerates every required frame, breakpoint, interaction detail, WCAG-AA guidance, annotation pattern, and a step-by-step implementation checklist.\\\\n\\\\nNext Actions Needed\\\\n1. Provide the team (or myself) with edit rights / API token for the Figma file “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” page 02_Admin so the wireframes can be drawn and commented.\\\\n2. Once access is granted, I will translate the stored specification into concrete Figma frames, add numbered annotations per checklist, comment “Admin UI v1 complete”, and share the updated link here.\\\\n\\\\nBlocking Issue\\\\n• Absence of Figma write permissions prevents creation of the actual mock-up frames; everything else is prepared and scoped in DOC-000002.\\\\n\\\\nPlease advise on granting the required Figma access or reallocating frame-creation to someone who already has it.\\\\n\\\\nThank you.\\\",\\n  \\\"originalOrderSequenceID\\\": \\\"ADM_001\\\"\\n}\\n###report_ADM_001###\"}]},{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"text\":\"###ADM_002###\\nSYSTEM MESSAGE\\nFrom: Seratt (8)\\nTo: 10\\nType: Orders\\n##\\n{\\n  \\\"text\\\": \\\"[Orders from Seratt (8) to Vorne (10) with sequence ID ADM_002:\\\\n\\\\n]FOLLOW-UP / UNBLOCKING ANSWERS – ADM_001\\\\n\\\\n1. Figma Access\\\\n   • Edit rights to page 02_Admin in “HOA Website – Low-Fi / Admin / Style Guide” have been provided. Work directly in Figma; API automation not required.\\\\n\\\\n2. Component Library / Grid\\\\n   • Use fresh grey-box placeholders; adhere to base 4-pt spacing grid and 8-column tablet / 12-column desktop layout. Style-Guide attendant will later skin components.\\\\n\\\\n3. Content / Sample Data\\\\n   • Populate the List View with 6 sample posts:\\\\n        – Spring Community Clean-Up\\\\n        – Annual Picnic – July 15\\\\n        – Board Election Results\\\\n        – Holiday Decorating Contest\\\\n        – Neighborhood Yard Sale\\\\n        – Pool Opening Schedule\\\\n   • Status badges: Draft, Scheduled, Published.\\\\n\\\\nProceed to translate your stored specification (DOC-000002) into concrete frames. Comment “Admin UI v1 complete” when done and reply with updated link.\\\"\\n}\\n###ADM_002###\"}]}],\"config\":{},\"tools\":[],\"output\":{}}}","genkit:state":"success"},"displayName":"generate","links":[],"instrumentationLibrary":{"name":"genkit-tracer","version":"v1"},"spanKind":"INTERNAL","sameProcessAsParentSpan":{"value":true},"status":{"code":0},"timeEvents":{"timeEvent":[]}}},"displayName":"generate","startTime":1753486775990,"endTime":1753486803859.605}